%S A400853 24,120,168,96,264,840,408,456,1320,552,672,696,1848,384,2280,1056,
%T A400853 1176,2760,1128,2856,1272,648,0,1416,9240,768,3864,1704,1824,1896,
%U A400853 4488,1992,5880,2208,5016,5640,2688,3000,2472,0,2568,3240,6072,2712,7080,7392,3048,1536
%N A400853 Largest number k such that the multiplicative group of squares modulo k is cyclic of order A396684(n), or 0 if no such k exists.
%C A400853 Write Q(m) = {k : the multiplicative group of squares modulo k is cyclic of order m}, then Q(1) = {k : (Z/kZ)* = (C_2)^r} = {divisors of 24}; for m > 1, k is in Q(m) if and only if (Z/kZ)* is of the form (C_2)^r X C_{2m}.
%C A400853 Factor m = p_1*...*p_s into prime powers. Then numbers in Q(m) are of the form d*q_{S_1}*...*q_{S_t}, where S_1, ..., S_t is a partition of {1, 2, ..., s}, q_{S_i} is a prime power such that (Z/q_{S_i})* = C_{2*Product_{j in S_i} p_j} or C_2 X C_{2*Product_{j in S_i} p_j}, and d is a divisor of 24 coprime to q_{S_1}*...*q_{S_t}.
%C A400853 Every term is divisible by 24, since lcm(k,24) is in Q(m) if k is in Q(m).
%H A400853 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400853/b400853.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400853 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852.txt">Examples showing the procedure to find Q(m)</a>.
%H A400853 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852_1.txt">List of Q(m) for the first 10000 values of m in A396684</a>.
%H A400853 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852_2.txt">PARI program for A400852</a>.
%H A400853 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplicative_group_of_integers_modulo_n">Multiplicative group of integers modulo n</a>.
%o A400853 (PARI) my(v); for(n=1, 200, if(is(2*n), v=multgp(n); print1(if(v, vecmax(v), 0), ", "))) \\ See _Charles R Greathouse IV_'s second program in A002174 and the PARI program link for multgp
%Y A400853 Cf. A400852 (cardinality of Q(m)), A400860 (smallest member in Q(m)).
%Y A400853 Cf. A396684.
%K A400853 nonn
%O A400853 1,1
%A A400853 _Jianing Song_, Oct 07 2026
